    "caseStudy": [
      {
        "heading": "Goal",
        "body": [
          "Build a job board backend the way a production API would be built: token auth, role-based access, caching, rate limiting, automated tests, documentation, and a real deployment pipeline."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Auth and access control",
        "body": [
          "Laravel Sanctum issues API tokens. A custom role middleware guards route groups for Candidate, Employer and Admin, so each role can only reach its own endpoints. Employers can only change their own jobs, and candidates can only withdraw their own applications."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Performance and abuse protection",
        "body": [
          "High-traffic read endpoints are cached in Redis. Named rate limiters give auth routes a strict 5 requests per minute and the general API 60 per minute."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Testing",
        "body": [
          "34 PHPUnit tests cover authentication, RBAC, jobs, applications and rate limiting. CI runs them against real MySQL 8.0 and Redis service containers, not mocks."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Delivery and cost",
        "body": [
          "MySQL, Redis and Apache run in Docker Compose on an AWS EC2 instance with an Elastic IP. On every push to main, GitHub Actions runs the tests and, if they pass, deploys over SSH: pull, composer install, migrate and clear caches. An EventBridge schedule starts and stops the instance to keep hosting cost down."
        ]
      }
    ]
